## Description

`SQLiteMemoryRepository.ensureVecTable()` is called lazily on every embedding write/search to make sure the `memory_vec` virtual table matches the embedding dimension. When the dimension does not match, it issues an unconditional `DROP TABLE IF EXISTS memory_vec`, deleting **all** previously stored embeddings:

```ts
private ensureVecTable(dim: number): void {
  const row = this.db
    .prepare(`SELECT sql FROM sqlite_master WHERE type = 'table' AND name = 'memory_vec'`)
    .get() as { sql: string } | undefined;

  const expected = `float[${dim}]`;
  if (!row || !row.sql.includes(expected)) {
    // Drop and recreate with the correct dimension
    this.db.exec(`
      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS memory_vec;
      CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE memory_vec USING vec0(embedding float[${dim}]);
    `);
  }
}
```

(`packages/infrastructure/src/database/sqlite.adapter.ts:95-109`). It is invoked from `store()` (line 136), `update()` (line 225) and `searchByEmbedding()` (line 173).

The schema seed creates the table as `embedding float[1]` (`sqlite.adapter.ts:85-87`), so the **very first** real embedding write already triggers a drop. More importantly, if the configured embedding model is ever swapped (e.g. 384-dim to 768-dim, or a config/provider change), the first operation silently wipes the entire vector index.

## Impact

Irrecoverable loss of all stored embeddings whenever the active embedding dimension differs from the table's current dimension. There is no migration, backup, or re-embedding path — the rows in `memory_entries` survive but their vectors are gone, so semantic search returns nothing until every memory is re-embedded. A routine model/config change becomes a destructive data-loss event with no warning. This justifies a critical / data-loss rating.

## Required Fix

1. Detect dimension state explicitly (e.g. read the actual dimension from `sqlite_master` once and fix it for the lifetime of the DB), and treat a mismatch as a fatal configuration error rather than silently dropping data.
2. If a dimension migration is genuinely supported, implement it as an explicit, opt-in operation that re-embeds or rejects existing rows, and guard it behind a transaction plus an operator confirmation.
3. Create the `memory_vec` table at the correct dimension on first use only when it is empty, never dropping a populated table implicitly.

## References

- CWE-212: Improper Removal of Sensitive Information Before Storage or Transfer (https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/212.html)
- CWE-440: Expected Behavior Violation (https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/440.html)

## Description

Every SQLite repository extends `SQLiteBase`, whose constructor unconditionally opens a brand-new connection to the given path:

```ts
class SQLiteBase {
  protected readonly db: Database.Database;

  constructor(dbPath: string) {
    this.db = new Database(dbPath);
    this.db.pragma("journal_mode = WAL");
    this.db.pragma("foreign_keys = ON");
    sqliteVec.load(this.db);
  }
}
```

(`packages/infrastructure/src/database/sqlite.adapter.ts:35-44`).

There are four subclasses — `SQLiteMemoryRepository`, `SQLiteTaskRepository`, `SQLiteSessionRepository`, `SQLiteEventRepository` — and each one calls `super(dbPath)`, so each instance opens a **separate** `better-sqlite3` connection to the *same* file. The application bootstrap already constructs two of them against `config.database.path` (`apps/agent/src/index.ts:40-41`), and a fully wired app would open four. Each connection independently re-runs `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS ...` and reloads the sqlite-vec extension.

## Impact

Multiple writer connections to one SQLite file contend for the database lock. Even in WAL mode there is a single writer at a time, so concurrent writes across repositories serialize on `SQLITE_BUSY` and — without a busy timeout (see related finding) — fail immediately. Reads on one connection may not see committed writes from another until a checkpoint, producing inconsistent reads. The connections are also never closed, so they leak file handles and WAL/SHM resources for the process lifetime. This is wasteful and a source of intermittent lock errors under load.

## Required Fix

1. Open a single shared `better-sqlite3` connection once (e.g. construct one `Database` and inject it into all repositories, or register a single connection in the DI container) and have every repository reuse it.
2. Initialise pragmas and load the sqlite-vec extension exactly once on that shared connection.
3. Ensure the shared connection is closed on shutdown.

## References

- CWE-404: Improper Resource Shutdown or Release (https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/404.html)
- better-sqlite3 (single-connection guidance): https://github.com/WiseLibs/better-sqlite3
- SQLite WAL concurrency: https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html

## Description

`AgentRuntime.runAgenticLoop()` enforces its execution timeout by `throw`ing from inside a `setTimeout` callback:

```ts
const timeout = setTimeout(() => {
  throw new AgentExecutionError(
    `Agent execution timed out after ${this.config.timeoutMs}ms`,
    agent.id
  );
}, this.config.timeoutMs);

try {
  while (iterations < this.config.maxIterations) {
    ...
    const thought = await agent.think(context);
    const action = await agent.act(thought);
    ...
  }
} finally {
  clearTimeout(timeout);
}
```

(`packages/core/src/usecases/agent-runtime.ts:130-198`).

The callback runs on its own macrotask, **outside** the `try/catch/finally` and outside any Promise chain. Throwing there does not reject the `runAgenticLoop` promise; it propagates as an uncaught exception. There is also no `AbortController` or cancellation token, so even when the timer fires, the in-flight `await agent.think()` / `agent.act()` / tool execution keeps running.

## Impact

When a slow `think`/`act`/tool call exceeds `timeoutMs`, the thrown `AgentExecutionError` becomes an uncaught exception on the event loop, which crashes the Node process (default `uncaughtException` behaviour). The surrounding `executeTask` try/catch (`agent-runtime.ts:72-113`) never sees it, so the task is not marked failed and no `task.failed` event is published. Meanwhile the original operation is never cancelled, so it can still complete (or hang) after the timer fired. A single slow LLM/tool call thus takes down the whole agent process instead of failing one task. This is a high-severity reliability/error-handling defect.

## Required Fix

1. Implement the timeout as a losing competitor in a `Promise.race`, e.g. race the loop body against a promise that **rejects** after `timeoutMs`, so the rejection is caught by `executeTask`'s try/catch.
2. Thread an `AbortSignal` into `agent.think`/`agent.act`/tool execution and abort it on timeout so in-flight work is actually cancelled.
3. Always `clearTimeout`/clean up the timer in a `finally` (already done) and make sure the timeout path marks the task failed and publishes `task.failed`.

## References

- CWE-248: Uncaught Exception (https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/248.html)
- CWE-703: Improper Check or Handling of Exceptional Conditions (https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/703.html)

## Description

The shared SQLite base sets only `journal_mode` and `foreign_keys`, with no `busy_timeout`, and exposes no `close()` method:

```ts
constructor(dbPath: string) {
  this.db = new Database(dbPath);
  this.db.pragma("journal_mode = WAL");
  this.db.pragma("foreign_keys = ON");
  sqliteVec.load(this.db);
}
```

(`packages/infrastructure/src/database/sqlite.adapter.ts:35-44`).

The application's shutdown handler simply flips a flag and calls `process.exit(0)` without closing any database:

```ts
const shutdown = async () => {
  console.log("\n\uD83D\uDED1 Shutting down...");
  this.running = false;
  process.exit(0);
};

process.on("SIGINT", shutdown);
process.on("SIGTERM", shutdown);
```

(`apps/agent/src/index.ts:86-93`).

## Impact

Without `busy_timeout`, any contended write (made more likely by the multiple-connection design in this file) fails immediately with `SQLITE_BUSY` instead of waiting briefly for the lock — producing intermittent, hard-to-reproduce write failures. Without a `close()` and without calling it on SIGINT/SIGTERM, the process exits while connections are open: pending WAL data may not be checkpointed, the `-wal`/`-shm` files are left behind, and there is no clean release of the database. Together these reduce reliability of the storage layer, especially under load or rapid restarts.

## Required Fix

1. Set a sane `PRAGMA busy_timeout` (e.g. a few seconds) when opening the connection so contended writes retry instead of erroring out.
2. Add a `close()` method on the repository/base (or on the shared connection) that calls `db.close()`.
3. Call `close()` from the shutdown handler in `apps/agent/src/index.ts` before `process.exit`, awaiting any checkpoint/cleanup.

## References

- CWE-404: Improper Resource Shutdown or Release (https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/404.html)
- SQLite busy_timeout: https://www.sqlite.org/pragma.html#pragma_busy_timeout

## Description

`EnvSecretsAdapter` reads secrets from an in-memory `overrides` map first and falls back to `process.env`, but its mutating methods only ever touch the override map:

```ts
async get(key: string): Promise<string | undefined> {
  return this.overrides.get(key) ?? process.env[`${this.prefix}${key}`];
}

async set(key: string, value: string): Promise<void> {
  this.overrides.set(key, value);
}

async delete(key: string): Promise<void> {
  this.overrides.delete(key);
}

async has(key: string): Promise<boolean> {
  return this.overrides.has(key) || `${this.prefix}${key}` in process.env;
}
```

(`packages/infrastructure/src/secrets/env.adapter.ts:17-31`).

`delete()` removes only the in-memory override; if the secret is provided via an environment variable, `get()`/`has()` still return it afterwards. `set()` writes only to the in-memory map, so the value is lost on restart and is invisible to anything reading `process.env` directly.

## Impact

Deleting a secret that came from the environment is a silent no-op — the secret remains readable, which is surprising for a `SecretsProvider` and can defeat rotation/revocation expectations. `set()` looks persistent but is process-local and ephemeral, so a written secret silently disappears on restart. Both behaviours violate the implied contract of the `SecretsProvider` port. Severity is low because this adapter is explicitly documented as an env-backed implementation to be replaced by Vault/Secrets Manager in production, but the silent semantics are still a bug.

## Required Fix

1. Make `delete()` either also unset the corresponding `process.env` key or, if that is out of scope for an env adapter, throw/clearly document that env-provided secrets cannot be deleted (fail loudly instead of silently).
2. Document `set()` as in-process/non-persistent (or persist it where appropriate), so callers do not assume durability.
3. Keep `get()`/`has()` consistent with whatever delete/set semantics are chosen so a deleted key is no longer reported as present.

## References

- CWE-672: Operation on a Resource after Expiration or Release (https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/672.html)
- OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet: https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secrets_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html

## Description

`AgentOrchestrator.processPendingTasks()` loads pending tasks, then for each one calls `delegate(...)`:

```ts
async processPendingTasks(): Promise<TaskResult[]> {
  const pending = await this.taskRepository.findPending(10);
  const results: TaskResult[] = [];

  // Sort by priority weight (higher = more important)
  const sorted = pending.sort((a, b) => b.priority.weight - a.priority.weight);

  for (const task of sorted) {
    const result = await this.delegate(task.name, task.payload, task.priority);
    results.push(result);
  }

  return results;
}
```

(`packages/core/src/usecases/agent-orchestrator.ts:74-87`).

But `delegate()` *creates a brand-new task* as its first step:

```ts
async delegate(taskName, payload, priority = ...): Promise<TaskResult> {
  // 1. Create task
  const task = await this.taskRepository.create({ name: taskName, payload, priority });
  ...
}
```

(`agent-orchestrator.ts:36-46`). So passing an existing pending task's fields back into `delegate` inserts a *new* row with a new id, executes that new task, and leaves the original pending task untouched (still `pending`).

## Impact

Each call to `processPendingTasks` duplicates every pending task in the store: the originals are never transitioned out of `pending`, while freshly-created copies are executed. Repeated invocations therefore multiply the backlog (the originals are picked up again next time, spawning yet more copies) and waste work re-running the same logical task under different ids. The pending queue is effectively never drained as intended. This is a logic bug; severity is low because the pending-task draining path may not yet be wired into a scheduler.

## Required Fix

1. Execute the already-persisted pending tasks directly — e.g. call `this.selectAgent(task)` and `this.runtime.executeTask(task, agent)` on each existing `task` instead of routing through `delegate()` (which is for creating new tasks).
2. Ensure the existing task is transitioned to `in_progress`/`completed`/`failed` (the runtime's `executeTask` already updates status by id) so it leaves the pending set.
3. Add a test asserting that processing pending tasks does not increase the total task count.

## References

- CWE-675: Multiple Operations on Resource in Single-Operation Context (related, via duplicate task creation) (https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/675.html)
- General guidance: queue draining should be idempotent and operate on existing records.

## Description

Suspected redundant `allSettled` usage in `InMemoryEventBus.publish()`:

```ts
async publish(event: DomainEvent): Promise<void> {
  const eventHandlers = this.handlers.get(event.type);
  if (!eventHandlers) return;

  const promises = Array.from(eventHandlers).map((handler) =>
    handler.handle(event).catch((error) => {
      console.error(`Event handler error for ${event.type}:`, error);
    })
  );

  await Promise.allSettled(promises);
}
```

(`packages/infrastructure/src/events/in-memory-event-bus.ts:12-23`).

## Impact

None — see rejection reason. Each handler is wrapped in its own `.catch()`, so failures are isolated and logged, and `publish()` awaits all handlers regardless of individual outcomes. The `allSettled` is functionally interchangeable with `Promise.all` given the per-handler catch, which is a stylistic nit rather than a defect.

## Required Fix

No fix required; behaviour is correct.

